UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 4: Oral presentation practice', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMake three cue-card lines for a bee research opener.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLines might be: question; why it matters; first section name — keywords, not a full essay.\n\nCHECK\nReading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPractise a 60–90 second research talk twice with cue cards. Record time, one eye-contact win and one wording fix for next rehearsal.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01","EN2-HANDW-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Practise a 60–90 second research talk twice with cue cards. Record time, one eye-contact win and one wording fix for next rehearsal. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using oral practice on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing.. Correction: Lines might be: question;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating oral practice on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with oral practice, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about oral practice with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for oral practice. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for oral practice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Lines might be: question","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Make three cue-card lines for a bee research opener. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for oral practice; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Lines might be: question","Careful use of cue cards and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing","Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with oral practice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named cue cards and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing","Name cue cards and explain why it matters only, without linking it to oral practice","Apply oral practice to new material and name the cue cards and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies oral practice with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries oral practice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing","Point to exact words in the sentence that show oral practice and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event"],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses oral practice from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name cue cards and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about oral practice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse cue cards and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for oral practice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about oral practice","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of oral practice on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about oral practice with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating oral practice on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using oral practice on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for oral practice. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with oral practice, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing.. Correction: Lines might be: question;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w36-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise oral practice.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use oral practice with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify cue cards and explain why it matters.","I can practise timing independently on a new prompt.","I can notice eye contact needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share.","options":["Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral practice with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake three cue-card lines for a bee research opener.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLines might be: question; why it matters; first section name — keywords, not a full essay.","explain":"Oral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Cue cards supporting a timed rehearsal.","caption":"Practise before the final share.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue cards</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">section headings only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Timing</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">60-second practice run</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Cards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Cards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral practice with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Eye contact contrasted with monotone page reading.","caption":"Keywords beat a script wall.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Eye contact</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">look up between points</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Problem</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">full-page monotone read</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral practice with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing. Here is a more productive model: Cards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLines might be: question; why it matters; first section name — keywords, not a full essay.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Reading a full page in a monotone is practice that still needs fixing. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral practice with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOral presentation practice uses cue cards, timing and eye contact so a research talk is clear before the final share. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 4: Oral presentation practice","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Research project and portfolio","week":36,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nCards show section headings only. I practise a 60-second run, look up between points and time myself twice.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Cue cards supporting a timed rehearsal.","caption":"Practise before the final share.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue cards</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">section headings only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Timing</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">60-second practice run</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Eye contact contrasted with monotone page reading.","caption":"Keywords beat a script wall.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y